      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14257592/assets/1fb4883a540f2c76f0aa98aa803f417ec1af5831e56f52be9491e4a75dd2b5e1.png)\n\nThe graph above shows a section of the function $y=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\pi <C<\\pi ,$ what is the value of $C?$",
      "worked_solution": "The constant $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function $y=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the given curve is a secant graph that's been shifted **right** by $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ units. So, the equation of the curve is:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\n$$\n\nTherefore, $C=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$",
